    "comments": [
      {
        "id": "comment-1",
        "text": "SPLITTING AND JOINING\n\nString Split cuts text at a delimiter and returns an ARRAY of strings; String Join glues an array\nback together with a separator. Array Length counts the pieces. Parse Int turns text into a number.\n  \"red,green,blue\"  --split on \",\"-->  [red, green, blue]  --join with \" + \"-->  \"red + green + blue\"\nClass variables hold single values (numbers, text, booleans), so the collection itself lives on the wires between nodes and only the answers are stored in variables.",
        "position": [
          60.0,
          -300.0
        ],
        "size": [
          900.0,
          210.0
        ],
        "color": [
          0.2,
          0.4,
          0.8,
          0.3
        ],
        "contained_node_ids": []
      },
      {
        "id": "comment-2",
        "text": "1. SPLIT\nString Split (delimiter \",\") turns \"red,green,blue\" into [red, green, blue]; the array is carried on a wire to the next nodes.",
        "position": [
          360.0,
          460.0
        ],
        "size": [
          560.0,
          100.0
        ],
        "color": [
          0.2,
          0.65,
          0.35,
          0.3
        ],
        "contained_node_ids": [
          "split"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "comment-3",
        "text": "2. JOIN AND COUNT\nString Join glues the pieces with \" + \" (\"red + green + blue\"); Array Length counts them (3). Both read the same array wire.",
        "position": [
          660.0,
          740.0
        ],
        "size": [
          640.0,
          100.0
        ],
        "color": [
          0.9,
          0.55,
          0.15,
          0.3
        ],
        "contained_node_ids": [
          "join",
          "len"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "comment-4",
        "text": "3. PARSE\nParse Int turns \"41\" into 41; add one: 42. String Parse Float does the same for decimals.",
        "position": [
